namespace pdg { /**
\fn pdg::Attributes::clipOverflow(boolean clip)
\brief Confine drawing overflow to the operation's bounds.

Defaults to false. When true, the operation temporarily intersects the current
Port clip with its transformed axis-aligned bounds, restoring the Port clip on
return. False allows operation overflow but never disables the Port clip.

Bounds are the supplied rectangle, quad or polygon bounds for closed shapes;
the ellipse or sphere bounding rectangle; measured text bounds for point text;
the supplied rectangle for rectangle text; and Drawing geometry bounds mapped
into the requested destination for Drawing replay. Line and spline bounds include
half the stroke width in Port pixels, so horizontal and vertical paths remain
visible; arc bounds use their ellipse with the same stroke allowance. Other
closed-shape bounds exclude the outer half of their stroke. Bounds follow
translation, rotation, scaling and reflection as an axis-aligned enclosing rectangle.

Images and image strips also crop fitted artwork to the destination before its
transform, including rotated destinations, subsection/frame selection and all fit
modes. fit_Clipped always crops; tiling crops partial edge tiles. Nested Drawing
replay respects its enclosing operation clip without exposing a public clip stack.

\param clip True to clip overflow; false to allow it within the Port clip.

\return This Attributes object, for chaining.
\see Port::setClipRect(), Port::resetClipRect()
**/ }
